; Claim: For measure-preserving (X,B,mu,T) mu(X)=1, the Kolmogorov-Sinai entropy h(T) = sup over finite partitions P of h(T,P), where h(T,P) = lim_{n->inf} (1/n) H(P v T^-1 P v ... v T^-(n-1) P) and H is Shannon entropy of the partition. h(T) is an isomorphism invariant (distinguishes systems the spectrum cannot, e.g. Bernoulli shifts by entropy - Ornstein). Variational principle: for a continuous map on a compact space, topological entropy h_top(T) = sup over T-invariant measures mu of h_mu(T); the maximizer is the measure of maximal entropy. Bridge to source coding: h(T,P) is exactly the Shannon entropy rate of the symbol sequence generated by coding orbits through P, so KS entropy = optimal compression rate of the dynamical symbol stream.
; Structure: (partition-entropy H(P)) (refinement-average h(T,P)=lim (1/n)H(join of pullbacks)) (KS h(T)=sup over P) (isomorphism-invariant, Ornstein) (variational: h_top = sup_mu h_mu) (equals Shannon rate of orbit-coding).
; Result: KS entropy is the measure-theoretic rate of information production per step; it upgrades mixing/ergodicity (qualitative independence) to a quantitative bit-rate invariant and connects topological, measure-theoretic, and coding notions of entropy.
; Bridge: genesis_mixing_dynamics (positive entropy is strictly stronger than mixing along the K-system chain) + genesis_birkhoff (Shannon-McMillan-Breiman is the entropy analogue of Birkhoff's pointwise theorem) + genesis_shannon_source (h(T,P) = Shannon entropy rate = optimal compression rate of the orbit-coding symbol stream).
